The code statements that lead to the bug are:
static void strp_abort_strp(struct strparser *strp, int err)
{
    cancel_delayed_work(&strp->msg_timer_work);
    if (strp->stopped)
        return;
    strp->stopped = 1;
    if (strp->sk) { ... }
}
Reason:
The function sets the `stopped` flag but does not release `strp->skb_head`. This leaves a reference to a partially assembled skb, causing a memory leak each time the parser is aborted (e.g., on message assembly timeout). Repeated aborts can exhaust memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (strp->skb_head) {
    kfree_skb(strp->skb_head);
    strp->skb_head = NULL;
}
strp->skb_nextp = NULL;
strp->need_bytes = 0;
Reason:
These statements free the partially assembled skb via `kfree_skb` and reset the parser state (`skb_nextp`, `need_bytes`) to prevent dangling references and ensure the parser can be properly re-initialized after an abort. This eliminates the memory leak.

